page('Table of Contents',listing=False);toc_anchor=doc.paragraphs[-1]._p
page('1 Introduction')
para('Laser Vault Heist is a complete first-person stealth exercise built around one compact mansion vault. The player reads the motion of six laser barriers, moves through the room, crouches below a final low beam and opens a mechanical safe. A laser contact changes the entire lighting state to red and offers a fresh attempt. This report explains the implementation, compares its methods with graphics literature and records reproducible local evidence.')
figure(S/'room-world-final.png','The original vault in the actual OpenGL runtime')
para('Figure 1 shows the visual target in the running application: warm wood and plaster, cold window light, restrained brass and a readable route. Geometry, UV textures, character rig and animation were authored in Blender. The renderer, lighting, scene hierarchy, collision and playback are custom C++/GLSL systems; no game engine performs these tasks.')
para('The outcome is a reproducible source project and a standalone Windows build with all three topic stretch goals: rotating spotlights, live CCTV and safe-opening victory. Evidence includes tests, a continuous captioned demo and three performance runs. A local result cannot establish performance on an unknown TA computer or replace a live demonstration of understanding.')
page('2 Literature Review and Background')
h('2.1 Selection and journal evidence')
para('Eighteen journal-indexed articles provide the background below. Titles, authors, venues and DOIs were checked against publisher-deposited Crossref metadata and publisher or author pages. Sixteen remain even if the two Communications of the ACM articles are excluded. Methods are compared with actual implementation choices; citing an alternative does not mean that it was implemented.')
table(['Journal','SJR 2025','Category'],[['ACM Transactions on Graphics','Q1','Computer Graphics and Computer-Aided Design'],['Computer Graphics Forum','Q1','Computer Graphics and Computer-Aided Design'],['IEEE Transactions on Visualization and Computer Graphics','Q1','Computer Graphics and Computer-Aided Design'],['Computers & Graphics','Q2','Computer Graphics and Computer-Aided Design'],['Communications of the ACM','Q1','Computer Science (miscellaneous)']],[2.8,.8,2.85])
para('The ranking year is 2025, not the historical publication year of every article. Quartiles were cross-checked through named public SJR reproductions listed with the references. Direct SCImago and Scopus access returned HTTP 403, so direct access is not claimed. Article identity is distinct from journal ranking provenance. Machine-readable records and both ranking links per journal are retained in references-verified.json and source-audit.json.')
page('2.2 Lighting and material models',2,False);review([1,2,3])
para('These papers separate a shading evaluation strategy, a physical reflectance model and an efficient approximation. Laser Vault Heist needs legible materials and a shader that can be explained line by line. It evaluates empirical Blinn-Phong lighting per fragment, with explicit diffuse color, specular strength and shininess. Brass is recognizable through shape, palette and highlights, but is not a measured conductor material.')
para('This choice also permits artistic control. Warm lamps and a cold moon source can be tuned without hiding the required dynamic illumination in baked textures. Physically calibrated reflectance would need new implementation and validation; the current shader is not described as PBR.')
page('2.3 Skeletal deformation and animation',2,False);review([4,5,6,16,17,18])
para('Together these works explain the selected compromise: a small authored hierarchy and sparse normalized weights give inexpensive GPU deformation. Fitting or dual-quaternion methods address quality limits that simple matrix blending does not remove. The project validates its limited authored poses rather than claiming a general deformation solution.')
page('2.4 Collision and visibility',2,False);review([7,8,13,9])
para('Visual complexity is separated from gameplay queries. Hundreds of decorative objects do not require hundreds of triangle tests. Large furniture uses simple spatial proxies, and each of six beam transforms supports a few upright body samples. A fixed 240 Hz simulation bounds ordinary motion between tests. This remains sampled detection: extreme motion or an unbounded timestep would require a continuous method.')
para('Static instancing addresses repeated visual work without an occlusion hierarchy. Measurements test whether that choice is sufficient on the available computer. They do not establish the same result for a larger room or the unknown TA machine.')
page('2.5 Display, shadows and texture sampling',2,False);review([10,11,12,14,15])
para('The design criterion is visible information at bounded cost. Glow makes thin laser geometry readable, one shadow map grounds the night scene, and mipmaps reduce aliasing. Global tone mapping does not model human adaptation, additive cones are not volumetric transport, and painted textures do not implement exemplar synthesis. These limits are retained in the evaluation.')
page('3 Project Description')
h('3.1 Objective')
para('The objective is to implement topic 12 as a playable, explainable computer-graphics project with its complete Core and all three Stretch goals. The player must fail, retry and win through the same rules used by the demonstration. Each feature should trace to inspectable assets, source code and a test or capture.')
h('3.2 Problem statement')
para('A thin rotating laser exposes several correctness problems. Its rendered orientation and contact volume must agree; a crouching camera must match the body proxy and animated mesh; a second camera must see the character without recursively sampling its own target. Movement must remain stable across frame rates, and the safe hierarchy must produce a coherent mechanical opening.')
h('3.3 Scope')
para('The room is approximately 8 by 12 by 4 metres. It contains wood flooring and beams, plaster and stone surfaces, a night window, deposit drawers, desk and chair, lamp, monitor, framed art, rug, plants and a grey safe with brass details. Six laser barriers occupy the route. The thief wears a brown jacket over a hooded zip sweatshirt, brown denim and low shoes, with visible skin and five-finger hands.')
para('The scope is one room and one player. Guards, combat, weapons, multiplayer, campaigns and shops are excluded. Sound was optional and is not implemented. This is the brief\'s single-author teaching example; the older PDF\'s team size does not justify invented participants or classroom history.')
table(['Interaction','Effect'],[['Desk lamp','Toggles local illumination and visible glow'],['Security monitor','Switches between two real CCTV cameras'],['Safe','Turns wheel, retracts bolts, swings door and wins']],[1.6,4.85])
page('4 Technical Implementation')
h('4.1 Tools and technologies used')
table(['Component','Role / verified version'],[['C++17 / GLSL 330','Game rules and own OpenGL shaders'],['GLFW 3.4 / GLAD 2.0.8','Window, input, context and generated GL loading'],['GLM 1.0.1','Vector, matrix and quaternion operations'],['Dear ImGui 1.91.9b / stb','UI, image decoding and screenshots'],['Blender 5.2.3 LTS','Geometry, UVs, rig, actions and export'],['Clang 23.1.4 / CMake 3.31.6','LLVM-MinGW 20261020, Ninja 1.11.1'],['Python / FFmpeg','Asset/evidence scripts and optional recording']],[2.5,3.95])
h('4.2 Development process')
para('Development progressed from an executable greybox and collision tests to Blender assets, skinning, rendering refinements and measured evidence. Actual Git commits preserve those changes; historical milestones and team contributions were not manufactured. Modeling/export scripts were executed through Blender MCP and retained. The final application runs without Blender or MCP.')
para('The course starter was not supplied. C++17 and CMake follow the brief\'s permitted baseline. All C/C++ dependencies are vendored; no download occurs during configuration, build or first run. Windows LLVM-MinGW was verified. MSVC and other operating systems have instructions but no claimed execution result.')
page('4.3 Algorithms and system architecture',2)
figure(FIG/'architecture.png','Data flow from input and exported assets to the displayed frame')
para('Figure 2 separates simulation from rendering. main.cpp collects input and manages the window. Game advances rules in fixed steps; world.cpp applies results to nodes and animation selection. Scene loads manifests and builds static instance groups. Shader, Mesh and RenderTarget own GL resources. Renderer schedules passes, Animator evaluates the skin palette, and UI reads the actual state.')
para('Interaction and retry edges are retained until a simulation step consumes them. Above 240 render FPS, a short edge can otherwise fall between updates. Pause stops simulation and animation. A minimized framebuffer waits for events instead of rendering to a zero-sized target.')
para('Menu, Playing, Alarm, Victory and Pause have explicit transitions. Retry resets position, laser phases, lamp/CCTV settings and safe timing. Director is a deterministic QA input producer; it cannot teleport the player or disable normal collision rules. The final demo is a reproducible live run through those rules.')
page('4.3.1 Transformations and laser contact',2,False)
para('With column vectors, world = parentWorld * T * R * S and clip = P * V * world * p. The rightmost operation acts first. Normals use transpose(inverse(mat3(world))) and normalize again per fragment, which is necessary under non-uniform scale. Parents precede children in the scene graph.')
figure(FIG/'local-box-test.png','An inverse transform reduces the oriented beam query to a local box test')
para('Figure 3 shows the world-AABB false-positive problem. For beam M and body sample p, q = inverse(M) * vec4(p,1). Contact requires |qx| <= hx, |qy| <= hy and |qz| <= hz. The implementation adds a 0.16, 0.055, 0.16 metre local margin and samples the body every 0.08 m from 0.12 m, plus its exact top. Rendering and collision share the beam matrix.')
p=para('glm::vec3 p = glm::vec3(glm::inverse(model) * glm::vec4(point, 1));\nreturn glm::all(glm::lessThanEqual(glm::abs(p),\n    halfSize + glm::vec3(1e-6f)));')
for run in p.runs:run.font.name='Consolas';run.font.size=Pt(12)
para('A left-wall pivot supplies the rotation centre. Five barriers move or rotate; the final 1.42 m beam requires crouching. Fixed 1/240 s steps reduce ordinary tunneling, but the sampled body is not an exact continuous collision solver.')
page('4.3.2 Animation and coordinate conversion',2,False)
pair(S/'character-rest-final.png',S/'character-crouch-final.png','Runtime T-pose and corrected crouch-walk pose in neutral lighting')
para('Figure 4 checks the same exported mesh in rest and crouched poses. It has 21,782 triangles, 20 bones and five actions: Idle, Walk, CrouchIdle, CrouchWalk and Interact. Weights sum to one; the format supports four influences and the assets use at most two. Local translation/scale lerp and quaternion slerp interpolate samples; transitions blend over 0.2 s.')
para('Skinning applies p\' = sum_i(w_i * G_i(t) * B_i^-1 * p), with current global bone G and inverse bind B^-1. The controller supplies world movement; clips remain in-place. Walk phase follows travelled distance using 1.2 m and 0.75 m strides. A crouched interaction overlays arm motion without raising the root.')
para('Blender (x,y,z) maps once to runtime (x,z,-y); transforms and inverse binds use C*M*C^-1. Native LVHM/LVHA formats document each field, while verified GLBs remain exchange assets. Body heights 1.90/1.29 m cover sampled mesh maxima 1.8909/1.2755 m.')
page('5 Graphics and Rendering')
h('5.1 Rendering techniques and live CCTV')
figure(S/'cctv.png','The physical monitor and enlarged view display a live second camera')
para('Figure 5 is a frame from the real-time demo. A 640 by 360 framebuffer has color and depth attachments; completeness is checked. Its camera renders the room and complete animated thief, omitting the monitor screen to avoid texture feedback. E changes the monitor camera. The main first-person pass hides the full body to prevent near-camera clipping.')
para('The renderer uses indexed VAO/VBO/EBO drawing and static instancing. Mesh/material groups receive their transforms once; dynamic safe parts, fixtures and bones update through uniforms. Textures and static mesh buffers are not reloaded each frame. Destructors release resources before the GL context is destroyed.')
para('Passes are a 2048-square moon depth map, CCTV, the main RGBA16F target, post-processing and ImGui. A separate display conversion makes enlarged CCTV suitable for ImGui. The physical monitor samples linear color within the main pipeline, avoiding a second gamma conversion.')
page('5.2 Lighting and shading',2)
figure(S/'spotlights.png','Rotating mounts, visible cones and surface spot lighting')
para('Figure 6 shows two spotlights whose positions and directions follow their mount matrices. Six other local lights and a directional moon illuminate the room. Local attenuation is power/(1 + 0.20d + 0.095d^2); smoothstep between cone cosines softens spotlight edges.')
para('For normal N, light direction L and view direction V, H = normalize(L + V). Per-fragment lighting uses base*max(N.L,0) + ks*max(N.H,0)^s, multiplied by color, attenuation and cone strength. Specular is suppressed on the unlit side. Ambient and emission are separate. The moon uses a biased 3 by 3 PCF depth comparison; local lights are unshadowed.')
para('This is empirical Blinn-Phong, not PBR [1]-[3]. Additive depth-tested cone meshes visualize dust; they do not integrate volumetric scattering. The HDR image receives modest glow and Narkowicz\'s credited fitted curve [19], followed by gamma conversion and vignette. It is not the photographic operator discussed in [10].')
page('5.2.1 Alarm and safe hierarchy',2,False)
figure(S/'alarm.png','Real contact changes illumination and feedback to red',6.15)
figure(S/'victory.png','The final attempt ends with the safe open and a real Victory state',6.15)
para('Figures 7 and 8 show failure and success in the same executable. Alarm recolors local lights, moon/ambient and emissive fixtures. Opening turns the wheel 150 degrees, retracts bolts 0.22 m and rotates the door 100 degrees around its hinge. Victory follows the sequence; retry resets it.')
page('5.3 Texturing and animation appearance',2)
pair(S/'character-face-final.png',S/'character-rest-final.png','Readable face and differentiated brown clothing in neutral runtime views')
para('Figure 9 verifies actual modeled skin, eyes, brows, nose, mouth and an open hood. The face is not a dark albedo mask. Jacket, sweatshirt, denim and shoes have distinct brown materials. Neutral inspection is brighter than the night scene so shape errors remain visible.')
para('Seeded offline image generation creates original painted wood, plaster, stone, cloth and leather textures. Blender exports UVs and corner normals. The game uploads PNGs, generates mipmaps and uses trilinear minification with suitable wrapping. sRGB decoding precedes linear lighting. This addresses the sampling concern in [14] without adopting the synthesis algorithm of [15].')
para('Pose checks sample all five clips, validate normalized weights and measure floor/height bounds. The lowest sampled point is 0.004 m above the floor. Visual review caught arms following the full torso lean in crouch; compensating the upper-arm rotation and bending elbows forward corrected it. Numerical validation and visual review cover different defects, within the authored motion range.')
page('6 Testing and Evaluation')
h('6.1 Testing methodology')
table(['Suite','Actual checks'],[['gameplay_math','Boundary/rotated point-box queries; AABB false positive; clearance; interaction, pause, alarm, retry and victory'],['complete_gameplay_route','Lamp/CCTV actions; deliberate alarm/reset; legal six-beam route at six initial phases'],['demo_and_slow_frames','210 s Director route; three interactions; retained short input edges; 6 FPS versus 120 FPS collision']],[2.15,4.3])
para('All three CTest suites passed in a clean Release build after unused vendor examples were removed. The executable ran from a different working directory and rendered the start menu and character review without GL errors. Actual frames were inspected at room, lamp, monitor, spotlight, alarm and victory milestones. GLB checks confirm isolated scene contents, a character skin and five named clips.')
para('The demo records real wall-clock frames, including deliberate failure and a real retry. Its uninterrupted final attempt follows normal movement and collision rules. A frame ledger and gameplay-state CSV connect media with actual transitions. This is automated integration evidence, not a fabricated manual playtest.')
para('Native UI automation could not obtain app approval, so human keyboard/mouse usability testing is not claimed. The TA hardware and exact course starter were not supplied. Live defense and author understanding require human assessment.')
page('6.2 Performance evaluation',2)
para('Hardware: Intel Core i5-13400F, NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5060, 32 GiB RAM, Windows x64 build 26200. OpenGL reports 3.3.0 NVIDIA 617.42. Three visible 1280 by 720 runs lasted 65 s each, excluding 5 s warm-up. VSync, frame limiting and video capture were off. Legal routes kept lasers, spotlights, CCTV, skinning, safe opening and retry active.')
table(['Run','Avg FPS','1% low','p50 ms','p95 ms','p99 ms'],[[b['run'],f"{b['average_fps']:.1f}",f"{b['one_percent_low_fps']:.1f}",f"{b['p50_ms']:.3f}",f"{b['p95_ms']:.3f}",f"{b['p99_ms']:.3f}"] for b in bench['runs']],[.45,1.1,1.1,1.1,1.1,1.6])
figure(FIG/'performance.png','Average and low-tail performance from the three raw timing CSVs')
para('Figure 10 uses average FPS = 1000 / mean(frame ms); 1% low = 1000 / mean(the slowest 1% of frame times). Percentiles use nearest rank. Whole-frame wall time includes simulation, draw submission, UI, logging and presentation; it is not isolated GPU time. Background desktop work was possible. Peak working sets were 109.7, 109.7 and 109.9 MiB; private memory peaks were 197.4, 197.9 and 192.0 MiB.')
para('Local results exceed the 30 FPS mandatory threshold and 60 FPS rubric target. They cannot close the TA-machine wording. Raw data, sample counts and method remain in evidence/benchmarks; unknown hardware receives no inferred guarantee.')
page('6.3 Issues and debugging',2)
para('High render rates exposed two timing bugs: short interaction edges disappeared before a fixed update, and animation alternated between Walk and Idle when a render frame contained no simulation step. Retaining edges and persisting actual movement speed resolved them. Regression tests protect the edge case.')
para('Early walk motion penetrated the floor. An analytic two-link leg solution now provides planted stance and lifted swing phases, with measured pose clearance. Neutral review also prompted the crouch-arm correction. Passing a weight-normalization check alone did not establish visual quality.')
para('GLB export initially included multiple open scenes. Selection filtering plus active-scene export isolated the vault and thief. The enlarged CCTV initially sent linear color directly to ImGui and looked dark. A dedicated display conversion fixed it; the physical monitor retained the linear path.')
para('An external desktop-capture experiment produced a white image instead of the OpenGL window and was discarded. The final recorder reads the real backbuffer into FFmpeg at wall-clock speed. Repeated sampling slots are logged explicitly; simulation is neither accelerated nor replaced with a manufactured success sequence.')
para('Remaining limits are explicit: the small shadow map can alias, local lights have no shadow maps, the body proxy is approximate, LBS can lose joint volume outside checked poses, and recording is Windows-only. These do not become claims of physically exact shadows, continuous collision or verified universal platform support.')
page('7 Conclusion and Future Work')
para('Laser Vault Heist combines a complete game loop with inspectable graphics implementation. The executable contains modern OpenGL, custom shaders and buffers, hierarchical 3D objects, dynamic per-fragment lighting, textured UV geometry, keyboard/mouse input paths, three interactions and a visible FPS counter. All three topic stretch goals operate in one room.')
para('Editable Blender sources, original rig/actions, verified exchange GLBs, native exports and scripts preserve the authoring chain. Collision, state and route tests passed. Continuous real-time video demonstrates failure, reset and safe-opening success. Three local performance runs show substantial headroom on the available RTX 5060 with a stated timing scope.')
para('The main lesson is consistency between systems. The same laser matrix drives rendering and contact; crouch state drives camera, collider and pose; color-space decisions agree across CCTV and main display. Independent subsystem correctness does not automatically create a coherent game.')
para('Future work begins with human input testing and the actual TA machine, then evidence-driven improvements: swept collision for faster motion, more stable shadow filtering, and stronger deformation for wider animation ranges. Accessibility controls or sound could be added if scope expands. Additional levels, combat and NPCs remain outside this submission.')
para('No oral defense, unknown-machine test, classroom milestone or grade is asserted. The repository, report, deck, demonstration and defense guide provide concrete material for those remaining human checks.')
